"Augustine taught that human nature itself has been damaged since the first sin, that every infant is born with a will already turned the wrong way, and that it cannot right itself without help from outside. He watched babies closely and wrote in the Confessions of the jealousy of an infant who glares at another child at the breast. The Buddhas remark on the infants latent tendencies makes the same observation. The interpretations are opposite. For Augustine the flaw is in the nature and only grace can mend it. For the Buddhist the flaw is a visitor and the mind can be cleared by its own cultivation. The Pure Land tradition, which holds that in this age one must rely on anothers power, is the nearest Buddhism comes to him.": "98fd1a98d39a",
"Because everyone has this capacity, no one can be written off, including the person you find hardest to bear.": "77317f833b3f",
"Buddha-nature does not mean a soul. It means that every mind is able to wake up.": "4fb8f006ce98",
"It is a reason for confidence, and it is no excuse for doing nothing. Gold that stays in the ore buys nothing.": "b258cea26381",
"Mencius holds that every person has the beginnings of virtue, that bad conditions cover them without destroying them, and that cultivation is the growth of what was always there. Ox Mountain, stripped by axes and cattle, is not bare by nature. The match with the nine similes is close in form. Something precious is present in everyone, it is hidden, the person does not know it, and the teachers task is to point it out. Both draw the same practical conclusion: no one is to be despised, since the sage and the fool have the same endowment. Chinese Buddhists saw the kinship at once, and the vocabulary of seeing ones nature is shared between the Chan schools and the later Confucians.": "d363cd807faa",
"Mencius is the closest. He and the Buddha-nature texts share four claims: the endowment is universal, it is covered and not destroyed, the person is usually ignorant of it, and practice reveals what was present. Rousseau shares the judgment that corruption is external and little else. Xunzi and Hobbes resemble the Buddhas remark about the infant and miss the other half. Augustine is the clearest contrast.": "8d4ce7413441",
"One old image is gold in ore. The gold does not have to be made. It has to be freed from what is mixed with it.": "c385e500ea98",
"Rousseau also holds that what we start with is sound and that corruption comes from outside. God makes all things good; man meddles with them and they become evil. The Buddhist term visiting corruptions could serve as a summary of his view. The difference lies in the source. For Rousseau the visitor is society, with its property, comparison, and vanity, and it arrived at a point in history. He therefore looks back to a lost condition. In Buddhism the defilements have no beginning, they are in the infant, and no institution put them there. Nothing has been lost. The radiant mind has never yet been uncovered.": "5d6475aa785c",
"The Buddha said that the mind is naturally bright, and that what darkens it is like a visitor who can be asked to leave.": "d00ab13fc283",
"The Chinese debate asked whether human nature is good or bad, and the European one asked whether we are innocent or fallen. The Buddhas analysis steps to one side of both. He describes a mind with no fixed nature at all. Greed, hatred, and delusion arise in it when their conditions are present and cease when the conditions are removed, and the same is true of generosity, kindness, and understanding. What is constant is that it can be trained. On this reading the statement that the mind is radiant is practical encouragement. It tells the practitioner that the work can succeed, as a goldsmith is told that the ore contains gold.": "cf939edcc3c5",
"The Mahāyāna gave the first claim a name and nine images. The Tathāgatagarbha Sūtra, composed around the third century, says: All sentient beings, though they are in the various realms of rebirth, have within their bodies of affliction the womb of the Tathāgata, forever unstained and complete in its virtues, no different from me. It is like honey in a tree, guarded by a swarm of bees. It is like gold dropped in filth, a treasure buried under a poor mans house, and a future king in the womb of a destitute woman who does not know what she carries. In China the idea became central. The Nirvana Sūtras phrase all sentient beings have Buddha-nature was taken as the final teaching, and in the Platform Sūtra the Sixth Patriarch, on awakening, exclaims: Who would have thought that our own nature is originally pure!": "898fb3c54d44",
"The early discourses contain a short text that later Buddhists returned to again and again. This mind, mendicants, is radiant. But its corrupted by visiting corruptions. An unlearned ordinary person does not truly understand this. And then: This mind is radiant. And it is freed from visiting corruptions. A learned noble disciple truly understands this (Aṅguttara Nikāya 1.51–52). Two things are said. The mind in itself is bright, and what darkens it has come from elsewhere and can go. The image is of guests in a house.": "0a7f122ba6d7",
"The likeness to Mencius ends at four places. First, what the endowment is. His sprouts are feelings, compassion, shame, deference, and approval, and they grow into social virtues. Buddha-nature is the capacity to awaken, and in many texts it is identified with the empty and knowing character of the mind itself. It is prior to moral feeling. Second, who has it. Mencius is distinguishing human beings from animals. Buddha-nature belongs to every sentient being, and some East Asian schools extended it to grasses and trees. Third, the self. Mencius assumes a self that cultivates itself. Buddha-nature texts that speak of a true self were at once reinterpreted by other Buddhists, who warned that the phrase is a teaching device for those frightened by emptiness. Fourth, the account of the obstruction. Mencius blames bad conditions and neglect. Buddhism blames ignorance without beginning, and it adds the infants latent tendencies, on which Mencius is silent and Xunzi eloquent.": "8c3594e666eb",
"The same discourses refuse the conclusion that people are therefore born pure. A monk once reasoned that an infant, having no concepts, could have no fetters. The Buddha replied that a baby lying on its back has no notion of sensual pleasures, yet the underlying tendency to sensual desire still lies within them, and no notion of other beings, yet the underlying tendency to ill will still lies within them (Majjhima Nikāya 64). These tendencies are called anusaya, things that lie alongside. The early teaching holds the two claims together. Nothing in the minds nature is defiled, and no one arrives undefiled.": "77621ac82be7",
"This is also why the tradition could affirm Buddha-nature and deny the self. If Buddha-nature were a thing inside us, a pure soul wrapped in dirt, it would be the ātman the Buddha rejected. The Buddhist philosophers who defended the teaching said that it names the minds emptiness of any fixed nature together with its capacity to know, and that the images of gold and honey are skillful means. A modern Japanese movement called Critical Buddhism has argued that even so the doctrine is a mistake, and that it encouraged East Asian Buddhists to affirm things as they are, injustices included. The argument continues. It shows that the question raised by Mencius and Xunzi, whether to trust what we find in ourselves or to work against it, is alive within Buddhism too.": "463ec2d48f0b",
"We are not born pure. A baby already has the seeds of wanting and of anger. But the seeds are not what the mind is.": "33ecd47e3071",
"Western listeners tend to hear Buddha-nature in one of two ways, and both mislead. Those from a Christian background hear a soul, a divine spark, and conclude that Buddhism has a self after all. Those drawn to self-help hear you are already perfect, and conclude that there is nothing to do. It helps to begin with the negative form: nothing that troubles the mind is built into it. Then give the positive form as a capacity: any mind can wake up. The comparison with original sin is useful if it is made carefully. The point of contrast is whether the flaw is in our nature. It is unwise to say that Buddhism teaches original goodness, since the tradition also teaches that we arrive with deep habits of greed and aversion.": "cc96eefadef7"
}
